WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.270
With such a massive cast of characters, Aquaman's
cinematic outing only had time to splash the

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.600
surface of some of these characters' incredibly
detailed backstories.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.860
As it turns out, a lot of the names and faces
in Aquaman have far greater importance than

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.880
you might've realized.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.060
Spoilers ahoy.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.020
It probably came as a surprise to non-comics
fans that not all Atlanteans are created equal.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.760
For example, only the high-borns can breathe
air, and practically nobody can talk to fish.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.040
For her part, Mera's unique power is the control
of water.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.860
In Aquaman, we saw some hints of that power
when she held back a tidal wave to save Aquaman's

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.740
father, but that only touched on the true
strength of her ability.

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:42.940
In Justice League #24, Mera went head to head
against the entire Justice League, and she

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.440
H2-owned them without breaking a sweat.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.680
And remember that time Frank Miller had Superman
come within inches of death after a nuclear

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:50.739
bomb filled the atmosphere with dust?

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.760
Mera went up against a nuke and saved herself
and Aquaman by putting them both in a bubble.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:56.640
Easy.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.940
Then in the Mera: Queen of Atlantis storyline,
she became you guessed it the queen of Atlantis

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:05.020
after throwing down against Ocean Master for
the throne.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.300
Kind of like what Aquaman did, but a whole
lot easier.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.520
Hail to the Queen.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.200
Aquaman the movie was largely inspired by
the Geoff Johns run on Aquaman the comic,

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.390
but the film made one major character change.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.400
In the comic series, King Nereus isn't Mera's
dad he's her fiance.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.520
Or at least, he is until she falls in love
with Aquaman, at which point he becomes her

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:25.720
lifelong enemy.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.260
Surprisingly, ol' Captain Redbeard hasn't
been a fixture of DC comics for very long.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.400
His first appearance was in the same Geoff
Johns-penned storyline, and he later emerged

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:38.630
from the comic book depths again to work with
Atlan to destroy Atlantis in the "Death of

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:39.620
a King" storyline.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.979
Despite being more of a sidekick to the villains
than the main villain himself, Nereus has

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.620
spent a lot of effort trying to get rid of
Aquaman.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.240
Guess that whole thing about having his fiance
leave him really got under his skin.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.939
For all the screentime Black Manta got in
the Aquaman trailers, the black-suited villain

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.540
showed up for precious little of the actual
film.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.259
After spending much of the beginning focusing
on Black Manta's origins and the start of

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:06.260
his quest for revenge against Aquaman, the
movie shifts gears to the main Orm vs. Arthur

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.820
plot, sidelining Black Manta as merely the
main bad guy's flunky.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.911
The film doesn't come close to exploring the
true relationship between Black Manta and

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.420
Arthur.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:19.440
A dig through comics history reveals that
Black Manta has been terrorizing Aquaman since

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.820
1967, concocting schemes up to and including
collapsing the Earth to create his own underworld

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:25.320
kingdom.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.280
If there's one character we would have loved
to see more in Aquaman, it would definitely

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:30.640
be Black Manta.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.500
Oh well, there's always the sequel!

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.480
This big-daddy Atlantean lived on-screen mostly
through flashbacks that showed the rise and

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:42.230
fall of Atlantis, but like just about everybody
in a comic book movie, there's a lot more

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.020
to him than that.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:48.700
In some of Aquaman's comic book origin stories,
Atlan is actually Arthur's biological father,

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:52.540
but even if you ignore all that and focus
on the Geoff Johns run on Aquaman, Atlan is

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.400
way more than a mere prop that holds a shiny
trident.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.140
As the Dead King, Atlan spent thousands of
years in hibernation before rising again to

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.320
destroy Atlantis.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.620
There are a lot of similarities between that
story and what we saw in the movie Aquaman

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:10.390
uses Atlan's trident to summon the Trench
and save Atlantis, and Arthur takes his rightful

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.500
place as king.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.900
Like most comics characters, Orm has gone
through a lot of changes over the years.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.040
For the most part, his story mirrors what
we saw in the film he's Aquaman's half brother

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.510
who wants to be king of Atlantis, and he hates
garbage.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.020
Pretty standard stuff for the fish people.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.260
But travel back a few years to the comic books,
and things get a lot more fun.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.170
In 2005's Retroverse storyline, for example,
Orm uses some of Arthur's bones to cast a

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.600
spell that reverses reality and turns Orm
into Aquaman.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:42.340
That's right Orm has magical powers, and he's
not afraid to use them on his half-brother's

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.360
disembodied hand.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.110
In the earlier Underworld Unleashed story
arc, Orm goes even further by selling his

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.960
soul to a demon in exchange for a magical
trident.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.220
The Joker, we should point out, sells his
for a box of cigars.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.080
That's the version of Orm we would have loved
to see, but apparently it was all too crazy

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.630
for the movie where people go to war riding
on fish.

